name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php
description: 'You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product

  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a

  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of

  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a

  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a

  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is

  read back tomorrow.

  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine''s identity is established,

  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment

  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at

  no machine, without a single line of them changing.

  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could

  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set

  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not

  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

  The steps below are one session and are worked in order: each builds on what the ones before

  it established, and none of them is a fresh start. Every one of them is bounded on purpose -

  give each what it asks for and stop there. The early steps are groundwork and are meant to be

  cheap; the audit in the middle is what this ticket exists for, and arriving at it with the

  clock already gone is how this comes out wrong. Short and complete beats long and cut off,

  everywhere here.

  '
steps:
- name: Orient in the codebase
  prompt: 'Task: get your bearings, cheaply. This step is groundwork and the ticket does not pay for it, so keep it short and do not go deep anywhere. First, the layout. What kind of product is this, and how is its tree divided up? Name the handful of areas a maintainer actually moves between, in whatever terms this codebase''s own structure suggests rather than in ones I have supplied, and give ONE file:line inside each that shows plainly what that area is for, plus a phrase in your own words on what it is for. One example per area, not a catalogue; five or six areas is plenty and the point is that you could find your way back to any of them. Second, fix the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of the remote machines this thing deploys onto, and the whole ticket is about that one type. Name it, give the file:line where it is defined, and say which of the areas above it belongs to. Do not map it here - that is the next step - and do not start listing what touches it. Third, one short paragraph: what conventions is this codebase written to, that a change to a core record would have to be made in keeping with? Say which of those you confirmed by opening something and which you are assuming from the shape of the tree. Work directly, in this session, yourself. No Explore agents.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '4'
    required: true
    description: The areas of the tree a maintainer moves between, each named once with one located example at file:line, plus the one type that stands for a remote machine located at its definition and placed in one of those areas
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_conventions
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_search
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Map the server contract
  prompt: 'Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later will send you back for it. Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a single subject rather than as a word to look for. Then two things about it, and the first is the larger. First, the identity itself, traced end to end. What identifies one of these machines; where the identifying fields are set as the record is written; where a lookup by identity is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store, what empties that cache and when; where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it, including any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; and what the removal path does, in order, and what it does not clean up behind it. Every routine you name gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without that location does not count. Second, and briefly: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as a roll. Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses - the store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none of that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the other direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE that type changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far side is the part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes that type''s name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller and easier part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed something a near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the type''s name anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step from the type. An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping there is the specific way this part gets failed. Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the second part of this ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the first half of this answer to keep meaning what it means today.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '10'
    required: true
    description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, cache, seed and tear it down, each located at file:line, plus the shape of the population a change to that type would land on - the areas it reaches, near edge and far side, named once each with one located example
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Trace the write path
  prompt: 'Task: trace how one of these machines comes into being and how it goes away, and be exact about what runs WHEN. This is still groundwork; the pass that this ticket exists for comes after it. Start at creation. Follow it from the point where something asks for a machine through to the point where the record exists and everything that has to exist alongside it does: what gates the write and refuses it, the write itself, and every further record the product seeds off the new one in the same breath. Give the exact file:line for each, and the routine it sits in. Then the other end. Follow removal the same way: what goes with the machine in the same breath, in what order, what is only marked rather than really removed, and what is simply left standing afterwards. file:line for each, with the routine. Then the part that the rest of this session turns on: for every step of both paths, say whether it runs INLINE, inside the request that asked for it, or whether it is handed off to run LATER and elsewhere. Name each deferred step at the file:line where the work is handed out, with its routine. Close with one short paragraph: which parts of these two paths would still be correct if a machine could be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and which would not.'
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '8'
    required: true
    description: The creation path and the removal path each traced end to end at file:line with the routine named, every seeded record marked as holding a LINK or a COPY, and every step of both paths marked inline or deferred with both ends of each deferred hand-off located
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
  prompt: 'Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the only question that matters after that: what breaks. Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place where the machine itself is not there and one identifying piece of it is - a piece lifted off a machine at some earlier moment by something else, and left somewhere the machine could never have been carried to. There are two fates for such a piece, and handing me only the first is how this part is failed. Either the piece comes back: it was parked in something that outlasts the moment, and later that same piece is turned back into a machine, and from there on the code trusts it to still mean the machine it came from. Or the piece never comes back at all: it was written in as a value of something else, or it was handed outside this product, and there is no lookup anywhere for the rework to break - what breaks instead is that the piece now says something untrue about which machine this is, and nothing here can reach it to correct it. Both fates are exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places changes by a single line when it does. The second fate is the half this pass usually loses, because nothing there looks like a lookup and nothing there fails loudly; go and get it. Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there, and the test has two halves. Is a piece being lifted off a machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to - held, written in, or handed out? Or is a piece being read back out of somewhere, after the machine was gone? Either one is in. If instead a machine came in and the code acted on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out. One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where the machine itself did not travel. How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either a piece is being kept there, and it gets the four parts below in full; or it is the ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you. For each kept place the answer has four parts and only four: the exact file:line where the piece is taken, kept, handed out or read back, and the routine that line sits in; THE OTHER END OF THE HAND-OFF, which is the exact file:line in a different routine where that same piece was lifted off an actual machine before it ever arrived here, and the routine that line sits in - if the place you are ruling is itself where the lifting happens, then the other end is the line elsewhere that later reads the piece back, and if you can genuinely find no other end, say which of the two ends is missing and why rather than leaving the pair half-written; which of the two fates it is, and with it how far the piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored record, or outside this product entirely and past correcting from here; and one phrase on what specifically starts being wrong the first time that piece stops meaning the machine it came from, saying plainly whether anything raises there or whether nothing does and it simply carries on. Keep it short: I want the coverage, the pair, the fate, the lifetime and the failure, not the prose. The pair is the point and it is not decoration. One end alone does not tell me whether anything still resolves: a line holding a bare value tells me nothing about which machine the value was taken from, or under what conditions, or whether the thing that took it is itself changing. A place given with only its own line is half a dependency and is worth half a place to me. And the two ends are almost never near each other - they sit in different areas, in code with different jobs, written by different people at different times, and the value travels between them by being handed on rather than by being named - so the ends of a pair cannot be read off each other and each one has to be established where it lives. Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with both ends of its pair located; then close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you genuinely run out of time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that point, rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no other end, no fate and no failure is a fragment of an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group with nothing in it.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place given as a PAIR of located lines, the one where the piece is kept, written out or read back and the one in a different routine where it was lifted off an actual machine, each with the routine it sits in, plus which fate its piece has, how long it outlives the machine, and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer means the same machine, with each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Trace the guards
  prompt: 'Task: trace how this product satisfies itself, BEFORE it acts, that the machine it is about to act on is a real one, still there, in a fit state, and one the caller is entitled to. There are several distinct mechanisms doing this and they are not interchangeable. One establishes that the thing exists at all. One establishes that the caller is allowed near it. One establishes that it is reachable and in a state fit to be worked on. One serves a repeat lookup out of memory rather than going back to the store, and something has to empty that memory when the underlying thing changes. Name each mechanism, and for each give the exact file:line where the decision is actually made, with the routine it sits in - the place that decides, not the place that calls it. For each mechanism, three things, and answer all three every time. What does it take as its input: a machine already in hand, or a piece of one that has to be turned back into a machine first? The second kind is the kind this rework reaches into, so be exact about which it is and locate the turning-back. What does it do when the answer is no - raise, return nothing, or carry on regardless - and say which of those is a silent pass that a caller downstream would never notice. And is it applied at every way into the behaviour it protects, or only at some? If only at some, locate a way in that skips it, at file:line. That last one is the most useful thing this step can tell me, so do not smooth it over. Asserting that a check is applied uniformly because it is the sort of thing that usually is is worth nothing here. If you did not look at every way in, say which ones you did look at and stop there rather than generalising.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '8'
    required: true
    description: Each distinct guarding mechanism located at the file:line where it decides, with the routine named, plus for each what it takes as input, what it does on failure and whether that failure is silent, and whether it is applied at every entrance with a skipping entrance located where it is not
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Assess the blast radius
  prompt: 'Task: rank. You now hold the places that kept a piece of a machine past the moment they had the machine, with both ends of each hand-off located. Turn that into a risk ordering a maintainer can work straight down. Do not re-derive the set here and do not add to it - this step is the ordering of what you already established. Give one ordered list, most dangerous first, every entry carrying the file:line you already established for it. Then say what you ranked on. The things that decide it: how QUIETLY it fails - whether anything raises at all, or whether it carries on and simply returns the wrong answer; how FAR the wrong answer travels before anyone could notice; whether the damage can still be corrected from inside this product or has already left it; and how OFTEN that path actually runs. A place that fails loudly on the very first request is a smaller problem than one that keeps working while being quietly wrong for a week, and an ordering that has them the other way round has ranked by how easy the code was to read rather than by what it costs. Then, for the dangerous end of that list, say for each what specifically must be re-verified before this rework ships: the exact condition that has to be shown to still hold, at the file:line where it would be shown. "Test it" is not that condition. A missed high-risk dependent is the one that pages someone. If there is a place you could not decide the risk of, put it at the dangerous end and say why, rather than dropping it out of the ordering.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '10'
    required: true
    description: One ordered risk list over the places already established, each entry at its file:line, ranked on how silently it fails, how far the wrong answer travels, whether it can still be corrected from inside this product and how often the path runs, with the exact condition to re-verify named for the dangerous end
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: The change and verification map
  prompt: 'Task: close the ticket out, concretely, in three parts. First, the edit. What you would actually change, as a list of edits, each at a file:line you have already established in this session, each with one line on what it becomes. Where the same edit repeats across many places, say it once - and still locate every place it lands, because an edit described once and located nowhere is not a change I can hand to anyone. Second, the map. The whole set you ruled in, grouped by area of the product, one section per area, every place at its file:line, and inside each section in the risk order you just established. This is the list a second maintainer works down while you are elsewhere, so a place that is in your ranking and missing from this map is a place that does not get changed. Do not summarise a section instead of listing it. Third, the verification. Two kinds, and say plainly which kind each item is. The automated checks that already exist and cover this behaviour today, each located at file:line, with one line on what about it would have to change. And the behaviours that have NO check covering them at all: for those, describe the check that would have to be written and what it would have to assert, and do not invent a location for it, because there is no line yet to point at. Finish with the single thing you are least sure of in this whole session, and the one thing you would read or run to settle it.

    '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '12'
    required: true
    description: The edits located at file:line, the whole ruled-in set grouped by area with every place listed separately at its file:line in risk order, and the verification split into existing automated checks located at file:line and behaviours with no check at all described rather than located
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3
gold:
- id: contract-model
  group: contract
  match:
  - Models/Server.php
  relation: 'app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine''s number, flushed by :222 `static::flushIdentityMap();` inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, with the flush itself defined at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void`; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of'
- id: c:server-setting
  group: contract
  match:
  - Models/ServerSetting.php
  relation: app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `public function server()` defined at :247 - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle
- id: c:server-delete
  group: contract
  match:
  - Server/Delete.php
  relation: app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:34 `public function delete($password, $selectedActions = [])` ending at :60 `$this->server->delete();` - the removal path, the moment after which every piece kept elsewhere resolves to nothing
- id: w:server-create
  group: write-path
  match:
  - Server/New/ByIp.php
  relation: app/Livewire/Server/New/ByIp.php:170 `$server = Server::create($payload);` inside `submit()` at :138 - the write itself, gated just above it by :142 `$foundServer = Server::whereIp($this->ip)->first();` which refuses a duplicate on the network ADDRESS, so the address is treated as a unique key at the moment the record is written; the seeding of every companion record fires off this one line
- id: w:seeded-copy-shared-var
  group: write-path
  match:
  - SharedEnvironmentVariable.php
  relation: 'app/Models/SharedEnvironmentVariable.php:22 `''server_id'',` in the fillable list opened at :11, with the back-link at :66 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `server()` at :64 - the seeded record that holds BOTH a link and a copy: app/Models/Server.php:191 `''value'' => $server->uuid,` and :199 `''value'' => $server->name,` write the machine''s handle and its display name into this record''s own `value` column as plain strings at creation time, and nothing re-writes them when the machine is renamed'
- id: w:seeded-destination-resolve
  group: write-path
  match:
  - Models/StandaloneDocker.php
  relation: 'app/Models/StandaloneDocker.php:135 `$server = Server::findCached($this->server_id);` inside `getServerAttribute(): ?Server` at :127 - the destination record seeded off a new machine (app/Models/Server.php:180-182) turns its stored number back into a machine through the per-request identity map rather than the store, so this is where a seeded LINK is resolved and where a stale number would yield null instead of raising'
- id: w:deferred-validation
  group: write-path
  match:
  - Server/ValidateAndInstall.php
  relation: 'app/Livewire/Server/ValidateAndInstall.php:63 `$this->dispatch(''validateConnection'');` inside `init(int $data = 0)` at :52 - the work that follows creation does NOT run inline: it is handed to a chain of later browser round-trips (`validateConnection` at :91, then `validateOS` at :143, `validatePrerequisites` at :157, `validateDockerEngine` at :194), so the machine is re-established from component state on each hop rather than carried through'
- id: w:teardown-deferred
  group: write-path
  match:
  - Server/DeleteServer.php
  relation: 'app/Actions/Server/DeleteServer.php:20 `$server = Server::withTrashed()->find($serverId);` inside `handle(int $serverId, ...)` at :18 - the far end of the removal hand-off: app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:60 `$this->server->delete();` soft-deletes the row and :61 `DeleteServer::dispatch(` hands only the NUMBER on to a queued action, which is why this end has to reach past the soft delete with `withTrashed()` to find anything at all'
- id: s:identity-map-flush-global
  group: specs
  match:
  - tests/Pest.php
  relation: tests/Pest.php:32 `Server::flushIdentityMap();` inside the global `beforeEach(function () {` at :27 - the per-request identity map is process-global state, so every test in the suite has to empty it by hand; any rework of how a machine is resolved lands on this single line for the whole suite
- id: s:identity-map-flush-feature
  group: specs
  match:
  - ResourceHostingServerTest.php
  relation: tests/Feature/ResourceHostingServerTest.php:26 `Server::flushIdentityMap();` inside `beforeEach(function () {` at :25, and again at :53 inside `afterEach(function () {` at :52 - a feature test that flushes on BOTH sides because it resolves machines by number across several API calls in one process; the double flush is the shape a test of identity resolution has to take today
- id: s:fillable-copy-regression
  group: specs
  match:
  - ModelFillableRegressionTest.php
  relation: tests/Unit/ModelFillableRegressionTest.php:55 `[ServerSetting::class, ['server_id']],` in the fillable table, with :56 and :57 asserting the same for the swarm and standalone destination records and :59 `[Server::class, ['ip_previous']],` pinning the previous-address field - this is the check that freezes which companion records are allowed to be mass-assigned a machine's number, so it is the check a rework of identity breaks first
- id: s:teardown-by-number
  group: specs
  match:
  - VultrDeleteServerTest.php
  relation: 'tests/Unit/VultrDeleteServerTest.php:72 `DeleteServer::run(` taking :73 `serverId: $server->id,` after :70 `$server->delete();`, asserting at :83 `expect(Server::withTrashed()->find($server->id))->toBeNull();` - the only test on record that exercises the deferred teardown through the NUMBER rather than the object, which is exactly the hand-off step 3 asks to be located'
- id: d:logs-ownership-check
  group: dependents
  match:
  - Project/Shared/GetLogs.php
  relation: app/Livewire/Project/Shared/GetLogs.php:127 `if (! Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->where('id', $this->server->id)->exists()) {` inside `getLogs($refresh = false)` at :125 - a component that already holds a machine throws it away and re-checks by its number on every poll, so the authorisation decision rides on the number rather than on the object it came from; the one row no baseline run on record has ever cited (0 of 4) and the sense arm has cited in 4 of 5
- id: d:additional-network-pivot
  group: dependents
  match:
  - Project/Shared/Destination.php
  relation: app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:130 `$this->resource->additional_networks()->attach($mainDestination->id, ['server_id' => $mainDestination->server->id]);` inside `promote(int $network_id, int $server_id)` at :115 - the number written into a pivot row as a column of the join itself, read back later to decide which machines a resource additionally runs on
- id: d:new-resource-state-id
  group: dependents
  match:
  - Project/New/Select.php
  relation: app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:409 `$this->server_id = $server->id;` inside `setServer(Server $server)` at :407 - the number kept in wizard state across every subsequent request of the flow and written into the created records later, while the machine object itself is dropped at the end of the request
- id: d:new-destination-state-id
  group: dependents
  match:
  - Destination/New/Docker.php
  relation: 'app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php:47 `$this->serverId = (string) $this->selectedServer->id;` inside `mount(?string $server_id = null): void` at :35 - the number stringified into component state and resolved back on a later request, so the machine is gone from its hands between the two halves of the form'
- id: d:compose-validate-id
  group: dependents
  match:
  - helpers/docker.php
  relation: 'bootstrap/helpers/docker.php:1252 `function validateComposeFile(string $compose, int $server_id): string|Throwable` resolving at :1255 `$server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($server_id);` - the helper''s whole contract with its callers is a bare int, so the machine is gone from every call site''s hands before this function starts and `find()` yields null rather than an error when the number stops resolving'
- id: d:ca-cert-job-id
  group: dependents
  match:
  - CaCertificate/Show.php
  relation: 'app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php:82 `server_id: $this->server->id,` inside `dispatch(new RegenerateSslCertJob(` opened at :81 - the number is peeled off the machine and handed to a QUEUED job by name, so the piece outlives the request that dispatched it by however far the queue is behind, and the job regenerates a certificate against whatever machine that number resolves to when it finally runs'
- id: d:proxy-config-state-id
  group: dependents
  match:
  - Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php
